-celani.jpg","pageCount":240,"libraryShelf":{"scheme":"lcc","callNumber":"RC569.5 .F3 C45 1994","rawCallNumber":"RC569.5.F3 C45 1994","mainClass":"R","subclass":"RC","completeness":"full_call_number","confidence":"medium","matchedBy":"open_library_work_consensus","sourceId":"source-open-library-lcc-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani","sourceEditionId":"/books/OL1104625M","sourceWorkId":"/works/OL3500999W","sort":{"classLetters":"RC","classWholeNumber":569,"classDecimalDigits":"5","cutters":[{"letters":"F","decimalDigits":"3"},{"letters":"C","decimalDigits":"45"}],"year":1994}},"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.356,"traits":[{"id":"academic","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Columbia University Press / Columbia University Press"]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"argues\"","catalog text contains \"theory\""]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Columbia University Press / Columbia University Press"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Women return to abusive partners because they are unconsciously drawn to them, driven by personality disorders linked to childhood abuse and neglect; effective treatment must target self-defeating relational patterns and fear of abandonment.","confidence":0.68},"academicOrientation":{"score":72,"confidence":0.42},"profileConfidence":0.78,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"a60f0a64d6f7e2c43f873b9a84020aa344240d1f44a8f126192123a1830637e1","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily addresses the psychological and emotional dynamics of battered women, focusing on the medical and mental health implications of abuse, which are central to the discussion.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"1688855987bc9769e3f64482a14d7c827aadcd6315e6891c9b89f10b68ff5520","summary":"The Illusion of Love challenges the prevailing model, which views the victim of abuse as a normal woman who is unable to escape from her batterer due to the effects of terror and psychological collapse. Instead, David Celani offers a new answer - that women who are battered have a fundamental attraction to partners who are abusive. Based on his years of clinical experience treating battered women, Celani applies object relations theory and case examples from his own practice to show that many women - and indeed some men - are unconsciously drawn to abusive partners because of personality disorders caused by childhood abuse and neglect. He argues that any effective treatment for battered women must help to unravel futile and self-defeating patterns, such as ones that spring from fears of abandonment and fascination with men who produce exaggerated promises of love followed by extreme rejecting behaviors. The Illusion of Love examines the personalities of abusers as well, many of whom suffer from narcissism, a disorder that is also often associated with childhood abuse and neglect. Narcissistic men lash out violently in an attempt to control their own fears of abandonment and to compensate for unsatisfied emotional needs. Celani concludes that domestic violence is often the tragic result of a union between individuals with complementary personality disorders.","publicationYear":1994,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Abused women","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Codependency","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Object relations (Psychoanalysis)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychology","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Attachment behavior","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Dependency (psychology)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Battered Women","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Object Attachment","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychoanalysis","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychotherapy","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychological Codependency","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-illusion-of-love-why-the-battered-woman-returns-to-her-abuser-david-p-celani"}],"displaySummary":"The Illusion of Love challenges the prevailing model, which views the victim of abuse as a normal woman who is unable to escape from her batterer due to the effects of terror and psychological collapse. Instead, David Celani offers a new answer - that women who are battered have a fundamental attraction to partners who are abusive. Based on his years of clinical experience treating battered women, Celani applies object relations theory and case examples from his own practice to show that many women - and indeed some men - are unconsciously drawn to abusive partners because of personality disorders caused by childhood abuse and neglect. He argues that any effective treatment for battered women must help to unravel futile and self-defeating patterns, such as ones that spring from fears of abandonment and fascination with men who produce exaggerated promises of love followed by extreme rejecting behaviors. The Illusion of Love examines the personalities of abusers as well, many of whom suffer from narcissism, a disorder that is also often associated with childhood abuse and neglect. Narcissistic men lash out violently in an attempt to control their own fears of abandonment and to compensate for unsatisfied emotional needs. Celani concludes that domestic violence is often the tragic result of a union between individuals with complementary personality disorders.","primaryTopic":"Drugs, Addiction & Treatment","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Medicine & Public Health","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Medicine & Public Health","mappedSubject":"Medicine & Public Health","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily addresses the psychological and emotional dynamics of battered women, focusing on the medical and mental health implications of abuse, which are c